{
'slug': 'google-ai-mode-local-business',
'cat': 'Search and AI',
'img': 'night-fog.jpg',
'title': 'Google says search is now AI search. Here is what local businesses should change.',
'dek': 'AI Mode is becoming the default way people search. The businesses that do well will be the ones that are easy to understand, not the ones with the most keywords.',
'body': '''
<p>At Marketing Live in May, Google said plainly that AI Mode is where search is heading, and coverage of the event put its audience at more than a billion monthly users. For anyone who has spent years thinking in terms of ten blue links, that is a big shift. The question for a clinic, a law firm or an interiors studio in Dubai is simple: what do I do differently on Monday?</p>
<p>The honest answer is less than the headlines suggest, but not nothing. The basics still matter, and a few of them matter a lot more than they used to.</p>
<h2>Fewer clicks, more decisions made on Google</h2>
<p>In AI Mode, people ask longer questions and get a written answer with a few businesses mentioned. Many of them will decide from that answer, or from your map listing, without ever opening your website. Your site is still the source, but it is no longer always the shop window.</p>
<p>This means the information Google can read about you has to be complete and consistent. Your opening hours, services, areas, prices or price ranges, and the languages you work in should be written clearly on your site and on your Google Business Profile. If those two disagree, you are asking a machine to guess.</p>
<h2>Write pages that answer full questions</h2>
<p>People type things into AI Mode that they would never have typed into a search box a few years ago, such as which clinic near Dubai Hills does same-week appointments for children. A page that answers that kind of question directly has a much better chance of being used in the answer than a page full of general claims.</p>
<ul>
<li>One page per service, covering who it is for, what happens, how long it takes and how pricing works.</li>
<li>A short list of the questions your team hears on the phone, with straight answers.</li>
<li>Your location details as plain text, not only inside a map or an image.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Reviews and mentions count for more</h2>
<p>When an AI answer recommends a business, it needs reasons it can trust. Recent reviews, replies to those reviews, and mentions on credible local sites give it those reasons. A profile with steady reviews from the last few months looks far safer to recommend than one with a burst of reviews from three years ago.</p>
<p>The practical step is to ask every happy customer for a review, at the moment they are happiest, with a link that takes two taps. It is unglamorous work, and it is one of the few things in search that is fully in your control.</p>
<h2>Stop judging search on traffic alone</h2>
<p>If more decisions happen on Google, your website visits may flatten even when search is working well. Watch calls, WhatsApp chats, direction requests and bookings that come from search and your map listing. Those numbers tell you whether people are choosing you, which is what traffic was always meant to stand in for.</p>
'''},
{
'slug': 'whatsapp-business-agent',
'cat': 'Automation',
'img': 'build.jpg',
'title': "Meta's WhatsApp agent is live everywhere. Should it answer your leads?",
'dek': 'The new Business Agent can answer questions, qualify leads and book appointments inside WhatsApp. It is useful, as long as you decide where it stops.',
'body': '''
<p>In June, Meta made its Business Agent available worldwide on WhatsApp, after nearly two years of testing in markets like India and Mexico, <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/03/metas-ai-agent-for-whatsapp-business-is-now-available-globally/">according to TechCrunch</a>. It can answer customer questions, recommend products, book appointments, qualify leads and hand a chat to a person when needed. For businesses in the Gulf, where so much selling already happens on WhatsApp, this is worth paying attention to.</p>
<p>The tempting move is to switch it on and let it run. I would slow down a little, because the agent will only be as good as what you give it and the limits you set.</p>
<h2>Where it clearly helps</h2>
<p>The biggest gap in most businesses is the evening and weekend reply. Someone messages at 10pm, nobody answers until the morning, and by then they have booked elsewhere. An agent that can reply straight away with real availability, prices and next steps closes that gap without hiring anyone.</p>
<p>It also takes the repetitive questions off your team. Opening hours, locations, parking, what a first visit involves and how payment works are the same questions every day, and they do not need a person.</p>
<h2>Where it needs a human</h2>
<p>Anything involving judgement, money disputes, medical or legal specifics, or an unhappy customer should go to a person quickly. Write those rules down before you switch anything on. A customer who is upset and gets a cheerful automated answer will remember it.</p>
<ul>
<li>Decide the topics the agent can answer, and the ones it must always hand over.</li>
<li>Set a time limit for the handover, so a person picks up within an hour during working hours.</li>
<li>Check a sample of conversations every week for the first month.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Feed it properly</h2>
<p>An agent that knows only your website copy will give vague answers. Give it the real material your best salesperson uses, such as price ranges, common objections and the questions that come before a booking. The more specific the source, the less it will make things up or fall back on generic replies.</p>
<h2>Keep the lead data</h2>
<p>Whatever answers the chat, make sure every conversation still ends up in your CRM with its source and outcome. Otherwise you will know the agent is busy but not whether it is booking anyone. Measure bookings that came through the agent, and how many handed-over chats your team actually closed.</p>
'''},
{
'slug': 'ads-in-ai-answers',
'cat': 'Paid ads',
'img': 'grow.jpg',
'title': 'Ads are moving into AI answers. What that means for lead generation.',
'dek': 'Google is putting conversational ads and a lead-qualifying agent inside AI Mode. The businesses that gain most will be the ones measuring bookings, not form fills.',
'body': '''
<p>The biggest ads news from Google Marketing Live this year was that ads are moving into AI Mode itself. Google showed conversational ads inside AI answers, and a Business Agent for Leads that can qualify someone in conversation and fill in the form for them. These formats run through AI Max for Search and Performance Max, so advertisers on classic setups will not see them.</p>
<p>For service businesses that live on leads, this changes a few things about how campaigns should be set up and judged. None of it requires panic, but some of it is worth doing before the new formats reach your account.</p>
<h2>The form is no longer the finish line</h2>
<p>If Google can qualify a lead in conversation and pre-fill your form, you will likely see more leads that look complete. That sounds good, and it might be. It also means a filled form tells you even less than before about whether someone is serious.</p>
<p>The only reliable answer is to track what happens next. Mark each lead as booked, not answered, not a fit or sold, and send those outcomes back to Google as offline conversions. That way the system learns from people who actually became clients, not from people who were easy to convert into a form.</p>
<h2>Your inputs matter more than your keywords</h2>
<p>AI Max and Performance Max decide a lot on their own. What you control is the material they work with: your landing pages, your assets, the questions your forms ask and the conversion data you send back. Weak inputs give the system nothing useful to learn from, however much budget you add.</p>
<ul>
<li>Landing pages that answer the obvious questions and show prices or ranges where you can.</li>
<li>Qualifying questions on forms, such as budget, timeline and location, kept short.</li>
<li>Offline conversion imports set up before you move budget into the new formats.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Test, do not jump</h2>
<p>Google shared figures on stage about extra conversions from these campaign types. Treat any platform number as a reason to test, not a promise. Move a portion of budget, keep a comparable campaign running, and compare cost per booking over a few weeks rather than cost per lead over a few days.</p>
<p>If the new formats bring bookings at a better cost, scale them. If they bring more leads but not more bookings, you have learned something valuable, and cheaply.</p>
'''},
{
'slug': 'ai-agents-booking',
'cat': 'Websites',
'img': 'glass-roof.jpg',
'title': 'When an AI agent books for your customer, can it book you?',
'dek': 'Assistants are starting to make calls and bookings on behalf of people. Businesses with clear information and simple booking will be easier to choose.',
'body': '''
<p>A new kind of customer is starting to appear, and it is not a person. Google has begun letting its assistant call businesses on a user's behalf in the US, starting with home services, and booking features are spreading across assistants and messaging apps. It is early, and most of it has not reached the Gulf yet, but the direction is clear enough to prepare for.</p>
<p>The useful way to think about it is this. When an assistant is asked to book a dentist or a property viewing, it will favour businesses where the information is clear and the booking is easy to complete.</p>
<h2>What an assistant needs from you</h2>
<p>An assistant cannot read between the lines the way a person does. If your prices are hidden, your hours are only in an image, or your booking depends on a back-and-forth on the phone, it has little to work with. Clear, structured information is what lets it say yes to you.</p>
<ul>
<li>Services listed with plain names, durations and price ranges.</li>
<li>Opening hours, locations and languages written as text and kept up to date on Google.</li>
<li>An online booking page that shows real availability, or a WhatsApp flow that offers times.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Simple booking helps people too</h2>
<p>None of this is only for machines. Every step you remove from booking also helps the person who is trying to book at 11pm on their phone. A booking flow that takes three taps instead of a phone call wins both kinds of customer.</p>
<p>It is worth testing your own booking from a phone, as if you were a new customer. If you get stuck, an assistant will get stuck too, and it will simply move on to the next business.</p>
<h2>Answer the phone, or make it obvious when you will</h2>
<p>If assistants are calling on behalf of customers, missed calls cost even more. Make sure calls are answered, or that a missed call triggers a WhatsApp message with booking options. Put your real response times on your site so nobody, human or not, is left guessing.</p>
<h2>Do not overreact</h2>
<p>Most of your customers will keep booking the way they do today for a while yet. The good news is that preparing for assistants is mostly the same work as fixing your booking for humans. Clear information, simple booking and fast replies pay off either way.</p>
'''},
